00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:07,560
and you're not going to leave just because I come in,

83
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:10,360
because you can't get back your sunk cost.

84
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:11,800
All right.

85
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:14,160
So, and then the entrant says,

86
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:15,920
well, given that you're not gonna leave,

87
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:18,040
if I come in, there'll be two firms,

88
00:03:18,040 --> 00:03:20,120
and in this simple model that we've set up,

89
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:22,120
that means that price will just get competed

90
00:03:22,120 --> 00:03:24,000
down to marginal cost,

91
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,920
and if you make, if you earn marginal cost,

92
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:29,440
and you had to pay a sunk cost to get in,

93
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:32,240
then how does your balance sheet look?

94
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:34,160
Bad, right?

95
00:03:34,160 --> 00:03:36,640
Everybody just loses their sunk costs.

96
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:40,640
So that is the, what happens when there's sunk costs.

97
00:03:42,120 --> 00:03:43,840
So if there's sunk costs,

98
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:46,840
and then the simple version is that

99
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:48,880
the entrant doesn't want to enter,

100
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:50,640
because he knows that in order to enter,

101
00:03:50,640 --> 00:03:52,120
he has to pay the sunk cost,

102
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:54,560
and then if he enters, you will be there,

103
00:03:54,560 --> 00:03:56,480
and then price will be driven down to marginal cost,

104
00:03:56,480 --> 00:03:57,760
you'll both lose money,

105
00:03:57,760 --> 00:04:00,760
and what is better for the entrant than losing money?

106
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:03,800
Not entering, right?

107
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:06,880
And if the monopolist knows that you're not going to enter,

