00:07:25,920 --> 00:07:27,320
it looks exactly like the story

198
00:07:27,320 --> 00:07:28,920
that I've just been telling you.

199
00:07:28,920 --> 00:07:30,840
So, that's one pure strategy

200
00:07:30,840 --> 00:07:32,640
and that's another one.

201
00:07:32,640 --> 00:07:34,600
Because if you were both there,

202
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:36,440
then someone would leave, right?

203
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:37,280
And if you're both there,

204
00:07:37,280 --> 00:07:38,600
someone would want to enter.

205
00:07:39,600 --> 00:07:41,760
But there's also the mixed strategy

206
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:43,040
Nash Equilibrium here.

207
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:43,880
And on your homework,

208
00:07:43,880 --> 00:07:44,720
you actually get to solve

209
00:07:44,720 --> 00:07:47,440
for that mixed strategy Nash Equilibrium.

210
00:07:47,440 --> 00:07:48,840
But if you just take a quick look at this,

211
00:07:48,840 --> 00:07:49,840
you might have an intuition

212
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:50,680
for what's going to happen

213
00:07:50,680 --> 00:07:52,120
for both of the possibilities.

214
00:07:53,560 --> 00:07:56,360
Namely, each side would actually,

215
00:07:56,360 --> 00:07:59,120
each side wants to enter if the other one doesn't.

216
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:00,200
If the other one does,

217
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:01,600
they don't want to enter.

218
00:08:01,600 --> 00:08:04,760
And then if each of them is randomizing,

219
00:08:04,760 --> 00:08:06,800
then there's actually going to be some probability

220
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:09,680
of all four of these situations occurring.

221
00:08:09,680 --> 00:08:10,640
Which makes sense,

222
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:11,480
because in the real world,

223
00:08:11,480 --> 00:08:13,880
we actually often see two firms

224
00:08:13,880 --> 00:08:18,240
go into a market where there's some costs

225
00:08:18,240 --> 00:08:20,360
and they both just sit there losing money.

226
00:08:20,360 --> 00:08:21,280
Happens a lot.

227
00:08:22,480 --> 00:08:23,960
In the first wave of internet startups,

228
00:08:23,960 --> 00:08:25,880
there were so many of them just entered

229
00:08:25,880 --> 00:08:27,600
and then lost a ton of money.

230
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:30,760
So remember there's like the original dot-com burst

231
00:08:30,760 --> 00:08:32,760
in 2000.

232
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:33,600
So there's that one,

233
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:35,400
like 2000, 2001.

234
00:08:35,400 --> 00:08:37,080
All right, so that's a scenario.

235
00:08:37,080 --> 00:08:38,560
And then sometimes people point out

236
00:08:38,560 --> 00:08:40,240
that you see situations

237
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:42,000
where it looks like there should be an entrant

238
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:43,840
and it looks like there's money to be made,

239
00:08:43,840 --> 00:08:45,280
and yet all the firms are there saying,

240
00:08:45,280 --> 00:08:46,600
I'm worried someone else is going to enter.

241
00:08:46,600 --> 00:08:48,600
I don't know if I want to go into that market.

242
00:08:48,600 --> 00:08:49,440
And the other firm's saying,

243
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:51,800
well, I don't know if I want to go in.

244
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:54,240
So sometimes people thought about,

245
00:08:54,240 --> 00:08:55,080
like there's like a place,

246
00:08:55,080 --> 00:08:57,120
so who isn't that perfect for a scheme resort?

247
00:08:57,120 --> 00:08:58,840
But the problem is if there are two scheme resorts,

248
00:08:58,840 --> 00:09:01,040
then they would both lose money.

249
00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:02,280
And then each firm may be saying,

250
00:09:02,280 --> 00:09:04,720
well, is the other one going to go in there?

251
00:09:04,720 --> 00:09:06,120
I don't know, I'm worried.

252
00:09:07,520 --> 00:09:09,160
And again, in the real world,

253
00:09:09,160 --> 00:09:10,480
this is complicated by the fact

254
00:09:10,480 --> 00:09:12,680
that there might be gradually rising demand.

255
00:09:14,080 --> 00:09:16,280
So maybe that right now you could lose money,

256
00:09:16,280 --> 00:09:18,480
but if you enter now and demand keeps rising,

257
00:09:18,480 --> 00:09:21,240
then you would get to be the incumbent 10 years from now.

258
00:09:22,240 --> 00:09:24,560
Or as you wait around, then it may be too late.

259
00:09:24,560 --> 00:09:26,240
So there's that as well.

260
00:09:26,240 --> 00:09:29,280
And this is a reason why you might want to start a business

261
00:09:29,280 --> 00:09:31,760
even though you know it's going to lose money for a while.

262
00:09:31,760 --> 00:09:33,440
If you see a market that's expanding

263
00:09:33,440 --> 00:09:35,200
and you think that there is,

264
00:09:35,200 --> 00:09:36,880
that you'll eventually will be able to enjoy that,

265
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:38,360
it's one of those situations.

266
00:09:38,360 --> 00:09:40,640
It's a reason to get in early.

267
00:09:40,640 --> 00:09:42,760
Of course, then you wind up losing a whole lot of money

268
00:09:42,760 --> 00:09:45,520
in order to capture those eventual monopoly profits.

269
00:09:48,160 --> 00:09:49,680
All right, questions?

270
00:09:49,680 --> 00:09:51,120
So I'm not going to solve this one.

271
00:09:51,120 --> 00:09:52,680
I'm going to leave you to solve it on the homework,

272
00:09:52,680 --> 00:09:55,880
but this is the setup and the intuition.

273
00:09:55,880 --> 00:09:56,720
Questions?

274
00:09:57,840 --> 00:09:58,680
Yes.

275
00:09:58,680 --> 00:10:02,800
Well, I guess in the first dot-com bubble situation,

276
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:04,200
it just seems like there's,

277
00:10:06,200 --> 00:10:07,760
the profits aren't known,

278
00:10:07,760 --> 00:10:10,760
and it seems like there was a whole complete gamble

279
00:10:11,800 --> 00:10:13,520
of who enters what.

280
00:10:13,520 --> 00:10:15,640
Right, although there's still the question of,

281
00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:16,560
whatever the profits are,

282
00:10:16,560 --> 00:10:19,240
do you really think there's room for two Amazons?

283
00:10:19,240 --> 00:10:20,080
Right.

284
00:10:20,080 --> 00:10:22,440
Which maybe, which of course, in 2000,

285
00:10:22,440 --> 00:10:25,080
I have someone say, yeah, why wouldn't there be?

286
00:10:25,080 --> 00:10:27,640
And yet it turns out that it didn't happen that way.

287
00:10:29,680 --> 00:10:31,360
So yeah, so I mean, of course,

288
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:33,400
these are all models where we're setting things up,

289
00:10:33,400 --> 00:10:35,240
where it's all clear what's going on.

290
00:10:35,240 --> 00:10:36,520
And if you want to say why the model

291
00:10:36,520 --> 00:10:37,360
doesn't work in the real world,

292
00:10:37,360 --> 00:10:38,200
you can just say people didn't know

293
00:10:38,200 --> 00:10:39,800
which model they were in.

294
00:10:39,800 --> 00:10:41,200
So there's always that.

295
00:10:41,200 --> 00:10:43,320
Although, once you have mixed strategy

296
00:10:43,320 --> 00:10:44,760
and Nash equilibrium, there's also,

297
00:10:44,760 --> 00:10:45,920
well, maybe they knew the model,

298
00:10:45,920 --> 00:10:47,720
but even the model itself predicts

299
00:10:47,720 --> 00:10:49,360
that there'll be confusion.

300
00:10:49,360 --> 00:10:51,640
Right, and basically that model says,

301
00:10:51,640 --> 00:10:53,120
people can understand the model perfectly,

302
00:10:53,120 --> 00:10:55,600
and yet people will still end up missing opportunities

303
00:10:55,600 --> 00:10:57,520
or sometimes losing money

304
00:10:57,520 --> 00:11:00,920
because the throw of the dice came out the wrong way.

305
00:11:00,920 --> 00:11:02,200
It's just like in a game of racquetball.

306
00:11:02,200 --> 00:11:04,520
Sometimes both players will go and bump,

307
00:11:04,520 --> 00:11:06,200
but we'll go and bump heads against each other

308
00:11:06,200 --> 00:11:07,040
and they'll hit their heads.

309
00:11:07,040 --> 00:11:08,200
And like, why did that happen?

310
00:11:08,200 --> 00:11:10,240
Well, we're each doing random strategies.

311
00:11:10,240 --> 00:11:13,800
And this was the case where my random strategy

312
00:11:13,800 --> 00:11:17,040
and your random strategy led to two bumps on the head.

313
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:19,400
So that's possible too.

314
00:11:19,400 --> 00:11:20,240
Yes.

315
00:11:20,240 --> 00:11:21,200
There were variants in your game too, I guess,

316
00:11:21,200 --> 00:11:22,720
for payoffs as well, right?

317
00:11:22,720 --> 00:11:23,560
We'll see.

318
00:11:23,560 --> 00:11:26,160
So, I don't think it's, not in this model,

319
00:11:26,160 --> 00:11:27,160
but yes, another way to get,

320
00:11:27,160 --> 00:11:29,400
but if you make them all richer, that's true.

321
00:11:30,280 --> 00:11:31,480
Okay.

322
00:11:31,480 --> 00:11:34,160
All right, so moving on.

323
00:11:35,560 --> 00:11:39,760
All right, so allocated versus productive inefficiency.

324
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:41,680
So most micro textbooks focus

325
00:11:41,680 --> 00:11:43,760
on the allocated inefficiency monopoly,

326
00:11:43,760 --> 00:11:46,760
this problem here of the bad triangles,

327
00:11:46,760 --> 00:11:48,240
the evil triangles,

328
00:11:48,240 --> 00:11:52,760
where you have price greater than marginal cost, right?

329
00:11:54,120 --> 00:11:54,960
Okay.

330
00:11:54,960 --> 00:11:57,520
And this is generally called allocated inefficiency.

331
00:12:02,720 --> 00:12:04,360
And if you're ever a bit confused,

332
00:12:04,360 --> 00:12:06,360
what is the intuition about what is supposed to be so bad

333
00:12:06,360 --> 00:12:07,960
about allocated inefficiency?

334
00:12:07,960 --> 00:12:08,880
I think we might've already done it,

335
00:12:08,880 --> 00:12:11,160
but it's worth doing repeatedly

336
00:12:11,160 --> 00:12:14,760
because it's so poorly, so rarely explained.

337
00:12:14,760 --> 00:12:16,960
So, you know, get two explanations in a lifetime

338
00:12:16,960 --> 00:12:18,520
and it'll still be worthwhile.

339
00:12:18,520 --> 00:12:20,880
So again, the intuition behind what's bad

340
00:12:20,880 --> 00:12:24,040
about allocated inefficiency is best expressed

341
00:12:24,040 --> 00:12:26,680
in the Steve Lansburg essay, Why Taxes Are Bad.

342
00:12:26,680 --> 00:12:29,360
And the idea of the essay is something like this.

343
00:12:29,360 --> 00:12:32,640
So suppose that you're willing to pay $20 for a shirt

344
00:12:32,640 --> 00:12:35,520
and the price without taxes is $18.

345
00:12:35,520 --> 00:12:38,280
All right, so do you buy the shirt?

346
00:12:38,280 --> 00:12:39,120
Sure.

347
00:12:39,120 --> 00:12:41,120
And how much consumer surplus do you get?

348
00:12:41,120 --> 00:12:41,960
$2.

349
00:12:41,960 --> 00:12:42,960
And how much revenue does the government raise

350
00:12:42,960 --> 00:12:44,520
with no taxes?

351
00:12:44,520 --> 00:12:45,360
Nothing.

352
00:12:45,360 --> 00:12:46,320
All right, now the government goes

353
00:12:46,320 --> 00:12:48,200
and imposes a tax of a penny.

354
00:12:48,200 --> 00:12:50,280
So the market price goes up to 18.01.

355
00:12:50,280 --> 00:12:52,040
Do you still buy the shirt?

356
00:12:52,040 --> 00:12:53,840
And how much consumer surplus do you get?

357
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:54,800
$1.99.

358
00:12:54,800 --> 00:12:56,480
How much revenue does the government get?

359
00:12:56,480 --> 00:12:57,800
Penny, right?

360
00:12:57,800 --> 00:13:00,840
All right, now the government imposes a tax of $1.99.

361
00:13:02,320 --> 00:13:04,880
So now, $1.99.

362
00:13:04,880 --> 00:13:06,480
So do you still buy?

363
00:13:06,480 --> 00:13:07,320
Sure.

364
00:13:07,320 --> 00:13:08,600
How much surplus do you get?

365
00:13:08,600 --> 00:13:09,440
Penny.

366
00:13:09,440 --> 00:13:11,000
How much revenue does the government get?

367
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:12,200
$1.99.

368
00:13:12,200 --> 00:13:13,960
Now, the fun part of the story.

369
00:13:13,960 --> 00:13:17,200
Government raises the tax up to $2.01.

370
00:13:17,200 --> 00:13:18,560
Price of the shirt goes up 21.

371
00:13:18,560 --> 00:13:20,160
Do you still buy?

372
00:13:20,160 --> 00:13:21,040
No.

373
00:13:21,040 --> 00:13:22,280
What happens to your consumer surplus?

374
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:23,920
What is your consumer surplus?

375
00:13:23,920 --> 00:13:24,760
Zero.

376
00:13:24,760 --> 00:13:25,600
Zero.

377
00:13:25,600 --> 00:13:26,440
What is the government's revenue?

378
00:13:26,440 --> 00:13:27,280
Zero.

379
00:13:27,280 --> 00:13:29,960
The tax has destroyed surplus.

380
00:13:30,960 --> 00:13:32,760
All right, so that is the idea.

381
00:13:32,760 --> 00:13:34,000
And of course, this isn't something

382
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:35,320
about government in particular.

383
00:13:35,320 --> 00:13:37,720
It's just something about price being greater

384
00:13:37,720 --> 00:13:39,600
than marginal social cost.

385
00:13:39,600 --> 00:13:41,800
All right, so same thing with monopolist.

386
00:13:41,800 --> 00:13:45,160
So the problem is not that you are charged,

387
00:13:45,160 --> 00:13:46,840
the problem is not that you're charging these people

388
00:13:46,840 --> 00:13:48,800
higher prices than marginal cost.

389
00:13:48,800 --> 00:13:51,800
The problem is that these people here don't buy anything.

390
00:13:51,800 --> 00:13:54,920
And that these people here lose the surplus,

391
00:13:54,920 --> 00:13:57,720
and the monopolist does not gain the surplus as profits.

392
00:13:58,600 --> 00:14:02,080
So that is what is supposed to be so bad

393
00:14:02,080 --> 00:14:03,480
about Alkerman inefficiency.

394
00:14:04,800 --> 00:14:06,160
Now, when economists started trying

395
00:14:06,160 --> 00:14:08,280
to actually measure these triangles,

396
00:14:08,280 --> 00:14:12,040
there were like a decade of being terrified of triangles.

397
00:14:12,040 --> 00:14:14,120
And then finally people started measuring them.

398
00:14:14,120 --> 00:14:16,160
There was a Chicago economist named Harberger

399
00:14:16,160 --> 00:14:18,240
who did one of the first main analyses,

400
00:14:18,240 --> 00:14:21,600
and it came out to a really, really small number.

401
00:14:21,600 --> 00:14:22,920
So he went and tried to calculate,

402
00:14:22,920 --> 00:14:24,320
what are all the deadweight costs,

403
00:14:24,320 --> 00:14:26,440
all the triangles in the economy,

404
00:14:26,440 --> 00:14:28,840
all the triangles that are keeping us awake at night.

405
00:14:28,840 --> 00:14:31,920
And he came up with something like 0.1% of GDP.

406
00:14:31,920 --> 00:14:32,760
And then it's like, well,

407
00:14:32,760 --> 00:14:35,400
so why do we worry about this problem?

408
00:14:35,400 --> 00:14:37,360
And of course, the obvious answer is,

409
00:14:37,360 --> 00:14:40,280
well, we worry about it because it's easy to teach in class,

410
00:14:40,280 --> 00:14:43,880
and it's geometry, and no one else has measured it.

411
00:14:43,880 --> 00:14:45,320
So put all that together,

412
00:14:45,320 --> 00:14:47,560
and of course we're gonna worry about a problem like that.

413
00:14:47,560 --> 00:14:49,880
So is the deadweight loss

414
00:14:49,880 --> 00:14:54,880
and the equal concept as the allocator, the inefficiency?

415
00:14:55,320 --> 00:14:56,160
I'm sorry?

416
00:14:56,160 --> 00:14:57,000
Deadweight loss.

417
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:58,320
Yeah, yes, the deadweight loss

418
00:14:58,320 --> 00:15:01,480
and the allocator inefficiency are the same in this model.

419
00:15:01,480 --> 00:15:02,320
Yes.

420
00:15:02,320 --> 00:15:04,680
All right, and then there were, of course,

421
00:15:04,680 --> 00:15:06,000
some other people who tried to go

422
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:07,680
and change Harper's assumptions

423
00:15:07,680 --> 00:15:09,600
in order to get a bigger number.

424
00:15:09,600 --> 00:15:11,480
So there was one guy in the early 70s

425
00:15:11,480 --> 00:15:13,320
who did a paper where he just assumed

426
00:15:13,320 --> 00:15:15,080
that every single industry in the entire economy

427
00:15:15,080 --> 00:15:17,680
is monopolized, and then what do you get?

428
00:15:17,680 --> 00:15:19,480
And then he got like 10% of GDP.

429
00:15:20,640 --> 00:15:21,560
Which is not trivial,

430
00:15:21,560 --> 00:15:24,000
but still he had to make a very extreme assumption

431
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:27,400
in order to get a number that seemed moderately scary.

432
00:15:27,400 --> 00:15:29,080
And I think that,

433
00:15:29,080 --> 00:15:32,760
oh yeah, so the final answer of the,

434
00:15:32,760 --> 00:15:34,840
the final word of the debate, Paul Samuelson just said,

435
00:15:34,840 --> 00:15:37,360
well, everything is small if you divide by GDP.

436
00:15:37,360 --> 00:15:41,400
So, although he shouldn't really wanna say that

437
00:15:41,400 --> 00:15:43,160
because he's been spending a lot of time

438
00:15:43,160 --> 00:15:46,680
talking about how bad the costs of recessions are,

439
00:15:46,680 --> 00:15:49,400
and those actually do stay large even if you divide by GDP,

440
00:15:49,400 --> 00:15:53,400
so contradicts sort of like his own broader message.

441
00:15:53,400 --> 00:15:54,760
But any case,

442
00:15:54,760 --> 00:15:56,640
I think that's a good point.

443
00:15:56,640 --> 00:15:59,880
But any case, there's the,

444
00:15:59,880 --> 00:16:00,760
and then I think there's a little,

445
00:16:00,760 --> 00:16:03,160
like the lamer response was,

446
00:16:03,160 --> 00:16:06,080
well, 0.1% of GDP is enough to buy every person

447
00:16:06,080 --> 00:16:08,560
in the country one stink dinner per year,

448
00:16:08,560 --> 00:16:09,680
and that's really good.

449
00:16:09,680 --> 00:16:11,680
All right, all right.

450
00:16:11,680 --> 00:16:13,360
Anyway, but there it is.

451
00:16:14,280 --> 00:16:15,120
Okay.

452
00:16:16,040 --> 00:16:17,200
And again, if you're trying to pull,

453
00:16:17,200 --> 00:16:19,080
why is it that they're so small?

454
00:16:19,080 --> 00:16:21,520
Well, the simple answer is that

455
00:16:21,520 --> 00:16:25,880
you're only losing surplus on the marginal units.

456
00:16:25,880 --> 00:16:27,760
Right, so this would be like

457
00:16:27,760 --> 00:16:29,400
the people that don't see Star Wars

458
00:16:29,400 --> 00:16:31,720
because the price is too high.

459
00:16:31,720 --> 00:16:33,040
Who are these people?

460
00:16:33,040 --> 00:16:34,200
Well, maybe like my mom.

461
00:16:34,200 --> 00:16:36,720
She's like, no, I'm not gonna really be paying $7

462
00:16:36,720 --> 00:16:38,040
to see Star Wars.

463
00:16:38,040 --> 00:16:41,600
All right, but losing the people who barely care

464
00:16:41,600 --> 00:16:44,480
does not actually add up to a whole lot of loss surplus.

465
00:16:44,480 --> 00:16:46,080
That is the intuition.

466
00:16:47,320 --> 00:16:48,400
All right.

467
00:16:48,400 --> 00:16:51,600
Now, far less discussed than productive inefficiency

468
00:16:51,600 --> 00:16:54,000
is another kind of inefficiency,

469
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,120
which is called productive inefficiency.

470
00:16:57,080 --> 00:16:59,080
Productive inefficiency.

471
00:17:03,640 --> 00:17:04,960
All right, so.

472
00:17:06,240 --> 00:17:08,720
Situation is productively inefficient

473
00:17:08,720 --> 00:17:10,880
if the average cost producing a given quantity

474
00:17:10,880 --> 00:17:12,760
is above the minimum average cost.

475
00:17:14,320 --> 00:17:15,160
Okay.

476
00:17:15,160 --> 00:17:16,800
All right, now, these productive inefficiencies

477
00:17:16,800 --> 00:17:19,160
can easily get very large.

478
00:17:19,160 --> 00:17:20,400
Okay, why?

479
00:17:20,400 --> 00:17:22,400
Well, because of the geometry.

480
00:17:22,400 --> 00:17:25,520
All right, so let's imagine we got a situation here.

481
00:17:25,520 --> 00:17:27,320
Let's make it easy where we've got,

482
00:17:27,320 --> 00:17:29,840
here's the marginal cost of an entrant.

483
00:17:29,840 --> 00:17:32,560
Here's the marginal cost of an incumbent.

484
00:17:32,560 --> 00:17:33,840
Here's demand.

485
00:17:33,840 --> 00:17:37,520
But the incumbent is the brother-in-law of the dictator.

486
00:17:37,520 --> 00:17:40,680
And so he goes to the dictator and says,

487
00:17:40,680 --> 00:17:43,800
brother-in-law, for the sake of our family's honor,

488
00:17:43,800 --> 00:17:46,800
could you please destroy that firm?

489
00:17:46,800 --> 00:17:50,120
Or put jail, the jail, or put them in jail,

490
00:17:50,120 --> 00:17:52,400
or whatever you want, and he's just fine.

491
00:17:52,400 --> 00:17:55,320
All right, but still, this is the minimum.

492
00:17:55,320 --> 00:17:56,680
Here we have constant cost,

493
00:17:56,680 --> 00:17:59,080
so marginal cost and average cost are the same.

494
00:17:59,080 --> 00:18:00,920
But yeah, so here is the minimum average cost

495
00:18:00,920 --> 00:18:02,200
of which you could produce.

496
00:18:02,200 --> 00:18:05,120
But here is the actual average cost we're producing.

497
00:18:06,200 --> 00:18:07,760
All right, now, just to keep it simple,

498
00:18:07,760 --> 00:18:11,160
imagine that the monopolist of the incumbent then says,

499
00:18:11,160 --> 00:18:13,680
and just to be nice, we'll agree to be regulated

500
00:18:13,680 --> 00:18:16,520
so we can't charge more than our costs.

501
00:18:16,520 --> 00:18:20,160
All right, so in this scenario, your price is there.

502
00:18:22,520 --> 00:18:24,160
So here, price is actually equal to market

503
00:18:24,160 --> 00:18:25,720
the marginal cost of the incumbent.

504
00:18:25,720 --> 00:18:28,520
So we don't have any of that conventional deadweight cost

505
00:18:28,520 --> 00:18:30,920
of allocated inefficiency.

506
00:18:30,920 --> 00:18:33,900
And if you go to the monopolist and complain,

507
00:18:33,900 --> 00:18:35,720
they could easily tell you this.

508
00:18:35,720 --> 00:18:37,940
Look, I'm not making any profit here,

509
00:18:37,940 --> 00:18:40,320
so you have no reason to complain at all.

510
00:18:40,320 --> 00:18:41,560
Society has not been harmed.

511
00:18:41,560 --> 00:18:42,400
How could it have been?

512
00:18:42,400 --> 00:18:43,600
I'm not making any money.

513
00:18:43,600 --> 00:18:47,720
All right, now, is that correct, looking at the diagram,

514
00:18:47,720 --> 00:18:49,640
that society has not been harmed?

515
00:18:49,640 --> 00:18:50,480
No.

516
00:18:50,480 --> 00:18:52,840
Is it true the monopolist is making no money?

517
00:18:52,840 --> 00:18:53,680
Yes.

518
00:18:53,680 --> 00:18:54,500
Yeah, so you're making no money,

519
00:18:54,500 --> 00:18:56,000
but they're still harming society.

520
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:57,320
How could that be?

521
00:18:57,320 --> 00:18:59,840
Because society lost the better deal

522
00:18:59,840 --> 00:19:02,480
that it could have had with the more efficient firm.

523
00:19:02,480 --> 00:19:06,560
And in fact, you say it has lost all of this here.

524
00:19:06,560 --> 00:19:09,680
And this, you may go back to geometry.

525
00:19:09,680 --> 00:19:11,920
Remember that something with this shape

526
00:19:11,920 --> 00:19:13,480
has a special name in geometry.

527
00:19:13,480 --> 00:19:14,560
It starts with a T.

528
00:19:14,560 --> 00:19:15,400
A trapezoid.

529
00:19:15,400 --> 00:19:16,680
A trapezoid, yes.

530
00:19:16,680 --> 00:19:18,880
The trapezoid of terror.

531
00:19:18,880 --> 00:19:20,360
So that is the trapezoid.

532
00:19:20,360 --> 00:19:23,880
All of this is lost because we have not allowed

533
00:19:23,880 --> 00:19:26,680
the most efficient firm, the lowest cost producer,

534
00:19:26,680 --> 00:19:27,640
to produce.

535
00:19:27,640 --> 00:19:31,920
And instead, he's been replaced with an incompetent firm

